  "full_review": "Hoping to be “Tootsie” on the basketball court, “Juwanna Mann” (Warner Bros.) shoots and misses as a weak fish-out-of-water comedy.\n\n����������� Jamal Jefferies (Miguel A. Nunez) is a Dennis Rodman-type character who thinks his talent affords him the luxury of being an obnoxious lout on the basketball court. Sure, Jamal can swish and slam dunk and sure, he’s the South Carolina Beats’ biggest scorer. But his antics have gotten out of control; one night he strips completely and parades his naked body in front of a packed game audience; prompting NBA officials to ban him from the league.\n\n����������� His attitude has gained him a reputation and no one wants him. Once his income dries up, his many possessions -- including a rack full of furs -- are repossessed and his prospects all disappear, Jamal finds himself living with his Aunt Ruby (Jenifer Lewis), running errands and playing bridge with her old friends.\n\n����������� Then one day, he’s hit with a bright idea. He’ll dress up in drag and try out for the women’s league. He lures his old agent Lorne (Kevin Pollak) to the audition, and after giving a phenomenal performance, Jamal, now Juwanna Mann, is offered a contract with the Charlotte Banshees.\n\n����������� He fools everyone -- including Lorne, who brokers the deal for him and whom he later takes into his confidence in order to keep up the charade. But Juwanna has the same bad habits as Jamal did -– hogging the ball, disrespecting his teammates and carrying the same bad I’m-the-center-of-the-universe attitude as before.\n\n����������� Although Nunez bears the weight of the film fairly well, looking very feminine in wigs and slinky dresses while affecting a Southern drawl, his performance cannot overcome the movie’s many flaws. Despite a few chuckles, the humor consists mainly of stale jokes regurgitated in the same hackneyed manner as seen dozens of times before.\n\n����������� Nunez is shoved into familiar scenarios –- managing to shower with the girls without revealing his true gender, narrowly escaping a physical required by the team, having his hairy legs painfully waxed and out on a blind date with clingy lothario Puff Smokey Smoke (Tommy Davidson with a mouth full of sparkly gold teeth). Although Nunez does his best, there is no escape from the stuffy familiarity with which these scenes are written.\n\n����������� Nor is there a respite from the predictable manner in which the film unfolds. At first, Juwanna and fellow teammate Michelle (Vivica A. Fox) clash over their distinctive styles. But all it takes is an extended one-on-one session to make them the best of buddies.\n\n����������� Juwanna starts to learn the value of teamwork and the Banshees move up in the ranks, finally making it to the playoffs. But romantic feelings begin to stir in Jamal for Michelle, who now considers him her best girlfriend.\n\n����������� The crude, sexually suggestive poses (Jamal is seen gyrating vigorously in several scenes), clothing and expressions in director Jesse Vaughan’s “Juwanna Mann” is also off-putting, not to mention unsuitable for adolescents. The sappy all-ends-well conclusion and feeble message about the merits of teamwork can’t compensate for what is essentially a derivative film chock full of old jokes with little entertainment value.\n\n����������� Due to several crude expressions and language and brief rear nudity, the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops classification is A-III-adults. The Motion picture Association of America rating is PG-13- parents are strongly cautioned. Some material may be inappropriate for children under 13.\n\n- - -\n\n����������� Navarro is on the staff of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops' Office for Film and Broadcasting."
